# Assignment 4 – Repository with at Least Three Meaningful Commits

##Introduction

Git is a version control system used to track changes in files and manage project history. GitHub is an online platform where developers can store and share their repositories. In this assignment, I created a Git repository and made at least three meaningful commits to track the progress of my project.

## Steps Performed

1. Created a project folder

I created a folder named `my-project` and opened it in Visual Studio Code.

2. Initialized the Git repository

I opened the terminal in VS Code and ran the following command:

bash

```
git init
```

This command initialized a new Git repository in the project folder.

3. Created project files

I created an `index.html` file containing the basic structure of a webpage.

4. Made three meaningful commits

I used the following commands to save changes to the repository.

Commit 1: Added the initial HTML structure

bash

```
git add index.html
git commit -m "Add initial HTML structure"
```

Commit 2: Added styling to the webpage

After creating a `style.css` file and linking it to the HTML file, I ran:

bash

```
git add .
git commit -m "Add CSS styling to webpage"
```

Commit 3: Added content and improved the webpage

After adding meaningful content to the webpage, I ran:

bash

```
git add .
git commit -m "Add webpage content and improve layout"
```

Each commit records a specific improvement to the project.

## Verification

To verify the commits, I used:

bash

```
git log --oneline
```

This command displays the commit history, including commit IDs and messages.

## Conclusion

Through this assignment, I learned how to initialize a Git repository, stage and commit changes, and view the project history. Meaningful commits help track progress, understand changes, and maintain a well-organized development workflow.
